{"id":17708,"title":"From Volume Rendering to 3D Gaussian Splatting: Theory and Applications","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/sibgrapi67909.2025.11223276","final_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.18101","year":2025,"kind":"paper","source":"openalex","doi":"10.1109/sibgrapi67909.2025.11223276","arxiv":null,"sha256":"0832b014bc4f2a7051840f8068c71527c07fbfff6405c9d26f15e2b7fcb2b846","ctype":"application/pdf","text_src":"oa","fetched_at":1793057879.685929,"text_chars":35750,"abstract":"The problem of 3D reconstruction from posed images is undergoing a fundamental transformation, driven by continuous advances in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS). By modeling scenes explicitly as collections of 3D Gaussians, 3DGS enables efficient rasterization through volumetric splatting, offering thus a seamless integration with common graphics pipelines. Despite its real-time rendering capabilities for novel view synthesis, 3DGS suffers from a high memory footprint, the tendency to bake lighting effects directly into its representation, and limited support for secondary-ray effects. This tutorial provides a concise yet comprehensive overview of the 3DGS pipeline, starting from its splatting formulation and then exploring the main efforts in addressing its limitations. Finally, we survey a range of applications that leverage 3DGS for surface reconstruction, avatar modeling, animation, and content generation—highlighting its efficient rendering and suitability for feed-forward pipelines.","file":null,"marks":[],"links":["https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.18101","https://doi.org/10.1109/sibgrapi67909.2025.11223276","https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/11223276","https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.18101"],"cited_by":[{"path":"1487-hardware-accelerated-isosurface-extraction-via-vertex-shader.md","name":"Hardware-accelerated Isosurface Extraction via Vertex Shader","k":1},{"path":"1477-point-tessellated-voxelization.md","name":"Point-Tessellated Voxelization","k":1},{"path":"2063-voxel-coordinate-mappi